  <static seed="1973" source="Contrib/PCC/BasicMath/Percent/MTH20PercentOfNumberType3_20.pg">
    <statement><p><m>3\%</m> of <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0001"/> is <m>15.3</m>.</p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>510</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><!-- PTX:WARNING: PGML wanted to make this a level 4 header. -->
<p>
Method 1</p>
<p>We will use proportion to solve this problem. Assume <m>3\%</m> of <m>x</m> is <m>15.3</m>, so <lq/><m>3</m> out of <m>100</m><rq/> corresponds to <lq/><m>15.3</m> out of <m>x</m><rq/>.</p>
<p>We will write and solve the proportion:</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle{\begin{aligned}[t]
   \frac{3}{100} \amp = \frac{15.3}{x} \\
   3x \amp = 100 \cdot 15.3 \\
   3x \amp = 1530 \\
   \frac{3x}{3} \amp = \frac{1530}{3} \\
   x \amp = 510
\end{aligned}
    }</m></p>
<p><m>3\%</m> of <m>510</m> is <m>15.3</m>.</p>
<!-- PTX:WARNING: PGML wanted to make this a level 4 header. -->
<p>
Method 2</p>
<p>We will use the percentage formula to solve this problem. This translation from English to math may help you remember the percentage formula.</p>
<p><m>2 \text{ is } 50\% \text{ of } 4 \iff 2 = 0.5 \cdot 4</m></p>
<p>The question is: <m>15.3</m> is <m>3\%</m> of what? Assume <m>15.3</m> is <m>3\%</m> of <m>x</m>. We have:</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle{ 
\begin{aligned}
        15.3  \amp  = 0.03 \cdot x \\
        \frac{15.3}{0.03} \amp  = \frac{0.03x}{0.03} \\
        510 \amp = x
\end{aligned}
    }</m></p>
<p><m>3\%</m> of <m>510</m> is <m>15.3</m>.</p>
<!-- PTX:WARNING: PGML wanted to make this a level 4 header. -->
<p>
Method 3</p>
<p>In the sentence <lq/><m>2 \text{ is } 50\% \text{ of } 4</m>,<rq/></p>
<p><ul label="disc">
<li>
<p><m>2</m> is the <alert>percentage</alert>,</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>50\%</m> is the <alert>rate</alert>,</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>4</m> is the <alert>base</alert> (following the word <lq/>of<rq/>).</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
<p>By the formula <m>\text{base} = \frac{\text{percentage}}{\text{rate}}</m>, we do a division to solve the problem:</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle{ \text{base} = \frac{15.3}{3\%} = \frac{15.3}{0.03} = 510 }</m></p>
<p><m>3\%</m> of <m>510</m> is <m>15.3</m>.</p>
<!-- PTX:WARNING: PGML wanted to make this a level 4 header. -->
<p>
Method 3</p>
<p>In the sentence <lq/><m>3\%</m> of what is <m>15.3</m>,<rq/></p>
<p><ul label="disc">
<li>
<p><m>15.3</m> is the <alert>percentage</alert>,</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>3\%</m> is the <alert>rate</alert>,</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><lq/>what<rq/> is the <alert>base</alert> (following the word <lq/>of<rq/>).</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
<p>By the formula <m>\text{base} = \frac{\text{percentage}}{\text{rate}}</m>, we do a division to solve the problem:</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle{ \text{base} = \frac{\text{percentage}}{\text{rate}} = \frac{15.3}{3\%} = \frac{15.3}{0.03} = 510 }</m></p>
<p><m>3\%</m> of <m>510</m> is <m>15.3</m>.</p>
</solution>
